// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// SITE RUNTIME — this machine, with tools from a container.
//
// The `local` profile runs every task here but sets no tool environment, so it
// assumes kraken2, bracken and the rest are already on PATH. This profile is
// the same executor with the software supplied by Docker instead, which is what
// makes it possible to run Phanta on a laptop or a CI runner with nothing
// installed but Docker.
//
// It exists for Phanta specifically. Phanta's four published tables have to be
// byte-identical to the upstream tool's, and every Phanta script writes through
// pandas.to_csv, whose float formatting is version-sensitive -- so a meaningful
// test run has to be in the SAME image the baseline was generated in, not
// merely in something with the right tools. See
// docs/phanta_migration_decisions.md section 2.
//
// It is deliberately NOT a general-purpose container profile. Only the Phanta
// processes get a container here; the bioBakery processes have none and would
// fall back to running on PATH, exactly as under `local`. Giving them images
// is the `amazon` profile's unfinished job, not this one's.
//
// No database paths, same as `local`. Pass them yourself:
//
//   nextflow run main.nf -profile local_docker \
//     --workflow phanta \
//     --phanta_samplesheet samples.csv \
//     --phanta_db tests/fixtures/phanta/tiny_db/db
//
// Build the image first:
//   docker build --platform linux/amd64 -t phanta:0.0.5-dev containers/phanta
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// A LOCAL TAG, not an ECR digest, and only here. Every other profile must
// reference the pushed digest: a tag is mutable, so two machines resolving the
// same tag at different times can get different images -- which is precisely
// the divergence byte-identity would otherwise hide. That is tolerable for a
// development run on one machine and nowhere else.
def phanta_image = 'phanta:0.0.5-dev'

// ── Resource ceilings ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// conf/base.config sizes every process for a cluster: kraken2 alone asks for
// 16 CPUs and 32 GB, because against Phanta's real 25 GB database that is the
// floor rather than a preference. check_max() caps those requests at the
// params.max_* values, and the defaults in conf/params.config are a cluster's.
//
// Left at the defaults here, Nextflow passes `-m 32g` to `docker run`, the
// Docker VM cannot satisfy it, and the container never starts: the task fails
// with exit status `-` and an EMPTY work directory -- no .command.err, no
// .command.log, nothing to read. It looks like a broken pipeline rather than a
// machine that is too small.
//
// So this profile caps at what a laptop or a CI runner actually has. These
// numbers are the ceiling, not a request: with the tiny test database kraken2
// needs a tiny fraction of either. Raise them on the command line
// (`--max_memory 16.GB --max_cpus 8`) on a bigger machine. Running the real
// 25 GB database here is not the point of this profile.
params {
    max_cpus   = 4
    max_memory = '6.GB'
    max_time   = '4.h'
}

process {
    executor = 'local'

    // One grouped selector, which conf/base.config does not use for any of
    // these -- so this adds a container without replacing base's cpus, memory
    // and time. See the two rules at the top of conf/profiles/harvard_rc.config.
    withName: 'kraken2|bracken|phanta_db_manifest|phanta_filter|phanta_scale|phanta_failed_samples|phanta_prepare_counts|phanta_total_reads|phanta_normalize_counts|phanta_merge_tables' {
        container = phanta_image
    }
}

docker {
    enabled    = true
    // Run as the invoking user, so published files are not left owned by root.
    runOptions = '-u $(id -u):$(id -g) --platform linux/amd64'
}
